<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilbour Papyrus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1145 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wilbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> is the largest known non-funerary papyrus from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, an administrative survey of cultivatable land made in the 4th year of Ramesses V, around 1145 BCE, in the late Ramesside Period. Ten metres long, it covers about 140 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> to near Tihna: Text A lists the holders of plots, among them priests, soldiers, ladies, herdsmen, stable-masters and farmers, and Text B the crown lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. It suggests that temples held 13 to 18 percent of Egypt's farmland, though whether the grain it records was tax or rent is unknown. Charles Edwin Wilbour bought it at Elephantine in 1893; it is in the Brooklyn Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilbour-papyrus.jpg" alt="Wilbour Papyrus" width="330" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo by unknown person in the Brooklyn Museum Ancient Egypt collection Original author was in Ancient Egypt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilbur_papyrus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings gave them fields, captives and the spoils of war, above all to Amun of Karnak, and modern reckonings from the Harris Papyrus and the <b>Wilbour Papyrus</b> put roughly 13–18 per cent of Egypt’s farmland in their hands under the Ramesside kings, most of it Amun’s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
